ABSTRACT="Vision-language models (VLMs) provide strong transferable representations
for visual recognition, but adapting them to downstream tasks remains
challenging when labeled data are scarce. Active learning (AL) addresses
this issue by selecting informative unlabeled samples for annotation;
however, recent VLM-oriented solutions, such as Calibrated Entropy-weighted
Clustering (CEC), select the final query from each cluster using a
nearest-center rule. This rule is representative, but it does not consider
whether a candidate is predicted to belong to a class with few labeled
examples. To address this limitation, we propose Three-Rank Calibrated
Entropy-weighted Clustering (R3-CEC), a parameter-free extension of CEC
that replaces the final within-cluster selection step with rank aggregation
over residual class coverage, informativeness, and representativeness.
Experiments on six image classification benchmarks across multiple
prompt-learning baselines show that R3-CEC improves over CEC in the main
setting across multiple budget settings, with gains of +3.2\%, +1.4\%, and
+0.2\%. These results indicate that R3-CEC is a simple and practical query
strategy for annotation-efficient VLM adaptation, with potential
applications in label-scarce visual recognition scenarios."
}
